;;; An `(absent …)` over a string always holds — regression
;;; `str-in-absent` (`corpus/regressions.toml`), the case's mechanism
;;; under `absent`.
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two `holds` facts, one over the atom `a` and one over the string `"b"`.
;;; `z` concludes `(hit z)` while `(holds a)` holds and `(holds "b")` does
;;; not.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A string is natural-language text, never a value (Q-M1e2.23), written
;;; only after `:why`, `:source` or `:goal-text`: the program is refused at
;;; the parse, at its first string, on every command.
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and what it answers ──────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `ff023413`, in a release build and a debug one, `z` fired: the rule
;;; compiler did not lower a string, so the pattern under `absent` was an
;;; opaque slot that matched nothing, and its `absent` always held. A
;;; conclusion the program refutes was in every state: `ein solve FILE
;;; --minimal` printed root's state as `(model (hit z))`. Today the program is
;;; refused at the parse: *unexpected `"b"`: a string*.

(relation holds Node) (relation hit Node)
(holds a) (holds "b")
(rule z () :match (and (holds a) (absent (holds "b"))) :assert (hit z))
